doc.add_heading('I. INTRODUCTION AND BACKGROUND', level=1)
doc.add_paragraph('Despite this, the architectural, engineering, and construction (AEC) industry continues to be one of the core pillars of modernization of the world’s economy, making up for 9-13 percent of global GDP. At the same time, this industry continues to be one of the most dangerous and technologically disjointed industries in the world. According to the world’s occupational safety institutions like OSHA (Occupational Safety and Health Administration) and ILO (International Labour Organization), deaths at construction sites are significantly higher compared to the ones at manufacturing sites, about three to four times. Struck-by accidents by falling scaffoldings, tools, overhead cranes, and impact injuries still stand out as the most common reason for fatal occupational trauma incidents. Accordingly, international legal standards mandate the mandatory use of Personal Protective Equipment (PPE), in particular hard hats (meeting the ANSI/ISEA Z89.1 or EN 397 standards) and retro-reflective high-visibility clothing (ANSI/ISEA 107 or EN ISO 20471) [17]. At the same time, project contractors, engineers, and facilities managers find themselves facing extremely complicated logistical and operational issues pertaining to work force management. Construction sites have very unstructured operational topologies consisting of temporary work forces, multi-levels of sub-contractors, decentralized perimeters, temporary physical structure, and tough environmental conditions such as dust, vibration, intense sunlight, and rain. In this tough industrial environment, the site managers must fulfill two critical operational conditions every day [15]:')

doc.add_paragraph('1. Deterministic Labor Entrance and Exit Attendance Monitoring: Authentication of the legally identified individuals and the total time of their shift in hours, along with calculation of statutory pay [14].')
doc.add_paragraph('2. Continuous and Dynamic Occupational Safety Auditing: Enforcement of strict compliance to mandatory PPE within high-risk areas in order to avoid catastrophic injuries and retain regulatory operating licenses. [17]')

doc.add_paragraph('Throughout time, the AEC industry has been handling these two concerns using manual operational procedures. The identification logging process has been conducted manually using clipboards through pen-and-paper as well as the manual management of punch clocks or the physical transfer of tokens under the watchful eyes of the gatekeepers. On the other hand, safety inspections have been done manually through the process of conducting inspections carried out by the patrolling Safety Officers. Many operational audits reveal that these manual processes have several failure modes. First, manual attendance sheets may be altered by the employees owing to mistakes, time stealing, unauthorized subcontracting, or the common practice of "buddy punching" where the authorized employee signs in for his/her colleague who did not show up [13]. To solve the shortcomings of manual supervision, the civil engineering and computer science literatures have looked at computer vision (CV) and edge artificial intelligence (AI) [1]. The first works were carried out in developing a computer vision model based on one single camera that captures optical video surveillance from entrance portals and staging areas where deep facial recognition is conducted to identify the individual while checking for the use of PPE [2]. Nevertheless, thorough field deployment has proved that this monolithic system is inherently flawed since it harbors a crucial structural weakness, which has been termed the Biometric Occlusion Dilemma [7]. In cases where workers follow all the safety guidelines by putting on their safety brimmed hard hats with the brims tightly pressed against the forehead, dust respirators for the mouth and nose, and tinted goggles for the area around the eyes, they end up eliminating over 70% of the spatial biological landmarks that are essential for deep facial recognition systems [8]. Therefore, False Non-Match Rate (FNMR) shoots sky high [9]. Workers are made to go through continuous false identifications and this makes workers unable to access the sites. This problem is further exacerbated by the fact that there is an inherent disconnect within the existing system between operational safety information and business administration within the enterprise. Although computer vision studies emphasize the need for optimizing the accuracy of detection algorithms on benchmark datasets, and studies on construction economics examine enterprise resource planning (ERP) systems, virtually none of the previous studies establish the link between digital validation at the field level and automated financial transaction [14]. In conventional sites, attendance sheets, safety violation sheets, and payment of subcontractors are done independently in different silos. There is a delay of 5 to 14 days in reconciling digital transactions with the weekly/daily payroll of subcontractors [14].')

p = doc.add_paragraph()
